  "letterId": "LTR-1862-06-13-002",
  "author": "henry",
  "date": "1862-06-13",
  "location": "Camp within five miles of Richmond",
  "excerpt": "I did not go over the battle ground afterwards, but the ground was covered with the dead.",
  "attribution": "Henry, to his brother Charles — Camp within five miles of Richmond · June 13, 1862",
  "stem": "Henry is describing the ground after Fair Oaks, outside Richmond. Why were Civil War battles this lethal?",
  "choices": [
   "Rifled muskets met tactics designed for older, shorter-range weapons",
   "Neither army took prisoners in 1862",
   "Artillery had become accurate beyond a mile",
   "Most of the dead were victims of hand-to-hand fighting"
  ],
  "answerIdx": 0,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. The rifle-musket was deadly at three times the range of the old smoothbore — but men still advanced shoulder-to-shoulder, as the manuals said. The arithmetic landed on fields like this one.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "1": "Both sides took prisoners throughout 1862 — parole and exchange were routine. The dead Henry saw fell to firepower, not to a no-quarter war.",
   "3": "Bayonet wounds accounted for well under one percent of casualties. The killing happened at range — which is exactly what made the old close-order tactics so costly."
  },
  "expansion": "The rifle-musket — a Springfield or Enfield throwing a conical Minié ball — was accurate to 300 yards and dangerous well past that, three times the reach of the smoothbores the tactics manuals were written for. But the manuals still ruled: close-order lines, massed volleys, frontal assault. The result was a fifty-year gap between weapon and doctrine, paid in bodies. Fair Oaks (Seven Pines) cost the two armies about eleven thousand men in two days of confused fighting in flooded woods — and it was only a preview: within three weeks the Seven Days battles would quadruple it. It was also the battle that gave the South Robert E. Lee, when Joseph Johnston fell wounded and command changed hands. The armies eventually adapted — trenches, skirmish order, the spade — but 1862 was the year the arithmetic taught itself.",
  "context": "Read who this letter is addressed to: Charles — brother to brother, not son to mother. In the same week Henry writes home about longing for a good meal; to his brother he writes 'the ground was covered with the dead,' plain as weather. The brothers kept a second, harder war for each other, and the split runs through the whole collection: reassurance flowed to Champlain, reality flowed between the tents. Henry has eleven weeks to live when he writes this. And Charles — the recipient — will enlist within three months of reading it, knowing exactly what the ground looks like. That's the fact worth carrying out of this question: the letters didn't just record the war; they recruited for it, honestly, inside one family.",
