  "location": "Camp within five miles of Richmond",
  "excerpt": "How I long to have one good meal at home, even if it wasn't anything but potatoes and salt, anything but hard crackers. I can not bear to look at them.",
  "attribution": "Henry, to Charles Hubbell — Camp within five miles of Richmond · June 13, 1862",
  "stem": "Henry, camped outside Richmond, is dreaming about food. What was the Union soldier’s actual staple diet?",
  "choices": [
   "Fresh beef and baked bread from field kitchens",
   "Hardtack crackers, salt pork, and coffee",
   "Canned goods — the war invented the ration tin",
   "Whatever the Sanitary Commission delivered weekly"
  ],
  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. Hardtack — a flour-and-water cracker hard enough to crack teeth — plus salted pork and coffee was the marching ration, weeks on end when the army moved.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "0": "Fresh beef existed when cattle herds kept up with the column, and soft bread appeared in fixed camps — but on campaign the haversack held crackers, not bakery.",
   "3": "The Sanitary Commission supplemented hospitals and camps with vegetables and comforts — a lifesaving margin, but never the ration itself."
  },
  "expansion": "The official marching ration was about a pound of hardtack, three-quarters of a pound of salt pork or a pound-and-a-quarter of fresh beef, plus coffee, sugar, and salt. Hardtack earned its nicknames — 'tooth-dullers,' 'worm castles' (the weevils were protein, soldiers joked) — and men crumbled it into coffee or fried it in pork grease into 'skillygalee.' The diet's real cost was invisible: months without fresh vegetables brought scurvy, night blindness, and the weakened constitutions that let camp diseases kill. The armies knew vitamin-C mattered without knowing why — 'desiccated vegetables' (compressed dried sheets the men called 'desecrated vegetables') and Sanitary Commission onion and potato shipments were the countermeasures. Diet, more than any single factor, is why disease outkilled battle two to one — and why in Act IV of this game, a veteran soldier outside Atlanta gets a diagnosis you might not expect in the world's best-supplied army.",
  "context": "'How I long to have one good meal at home, even if it wasn't anything but…' — Henry's hunger reaches for the family table, not a restaurant; the fantasy is domestic, specific, his mother's kitchen. He writes this to Charles the same day he tells him the ground at Fair Oaks was covered with the dead — homesickness and horror sharing one envelope, which is what June 1862 on the Peninsula actually felt like. There's foreshadowing here the family couldn't see: the diet Henry grumbles about will, two years later, put his brother Alexander in an Atlanta hospital with scurvy. When you reach that question in Act IV, remember this one — the same broken supply of vegetables, working on a different brother.",
