  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. “Waiting to see if there would not soon be some news of our going home” — after Appomattox, the war's correspondence ran on exactly one question, asked in every tent in every camp: when?",
  "whyWrong": {
   "0": "The armies' war was over — Johnston had surrendered in April. The negotiation that concerned a sergeant in June was between his regiment and a demobilization schedule.",
   "3": "The brothers did track each other to the end — but his held pen was waiting on HIS orders, the only news he could give her that counted."
  },
  "expansion": "Between Appomattox (April 9) and each regiment's muster-out stretched a season with no name — the war over, the army intact, a million men writing home variations of one sentence: no news yet of our going home. Demobilization ran by schedule and category (long-service regiments first), and the schedule was opaque from inside a camp, so rumor did its usual work at double speed; soldiers' letters of May–July 1865 are a genre of managed impatience, letters deliberately HELD (as Alexander holds this one) in hope of enclosing the only fact that mattered. Notice what the delay did to the correspondence itself: after four years of letters thick with battles, prices, politics, and sickness, the content thinned to logistics and longing — the war's great written conversation audibly winding down as its reason dissolved. The Hubbell collection's final pages are exactly this: short letters about schedules, money, and reunion, the archive documenting its own approaching silence.",
  "context": "'You have doubtless wondered at my long silence' — the apology every Hubbell letter-opener dreaded, deployed one last time for the happiest possible reason: he'd been saving the envelope for the word HOME. This is the June 16 letter's third appearance in this act (dissolution, hunger, and now homecoming — the war's whole ending in one page), and its family beat is the sweetest: after 272 letters of managing distance, the correspondence's final job is to schedule its own obsolescence. Six weeks later he's at Ogdensburgh promising the rest 'at the tea table,' and the record closes. One shadow for the reader who knows the whole story: the same going-home season that ends Alexander's letters is the one that takes James, en route from Savannah in October. The family got its reunion minus one — which is why the finale's words are what they are: two came home.",
