  "location": "Near Alexandria, Virginia",
  "excerpt": "Now by the providence of God, after four years of unparalleled struggling we stand a free and united nation, than which in point of honor we have no superior and in point of freedom, no equal.",
  "attribution": "Alexander, to his sister Fannie — Near Alexandria, Virginia · June 27, 1865",
  "stem": "A sergeant sums up what four years bought. In his words “free and united,” what two outcomes of the war is he naming?",
  "choices": [
   "Free trade and united markets",
   "Emancipation and the preservation of the Union",
   "Freedom of the press and a united party system",
   "Free land in the West and a united currency"
  ],
  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. “Free” — slavery destroyed, the Thirteenth Amendment through Congress and ratifying that very year. “United” — secession broken. A farm-boy sergeant fused the war's two great results into six words.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "0": "The economy transformed — greenbacks, railroads, tariffs — but that's not the register he's writing in. 'In point of freedom, no equal' is about human beings.",
   "3": "Homesteads and greenbacks were real wartime creations — but read his sentence again: honor and freedom are its stakes, not acreage."
  },
  "expansion": "The order of Alexander's adjectives is a historical finding in itself. In 1861, Union soldiers' letters overwhelmingly named one war aim: the Union — the Revolution's inheritance, 'the last best hope.' Emancipation entered the ranks' vocabulary gradually and unevenly: through the logic of war (every enslaved laborer freed weakened the Confederacy), through what soldiers saw marching south, through Black regiments' fighting record after 1863. Historians who track this evolution across thousands of letters call it the great measurable shift in the war generation's mind — and here it stands completed in one sentence from a farm-boy sergeant: 'free' FIRST, 'united' second, unprompted, to his sister. Not every veteran made that journey (this same archive holds letters that didn't), which makes the specimens that did the more valuable. The 272 words of the Gettysburg Address made the argument; sentences like this one, multiplied by a hundred thousand hands, are the evidence it landed.",
  "context": "Here is the full arc this game has been walking you along: January 1862, a seventeen-year-old defends his enlistment as more than novelty; June 1865, the same hand writes a valediction — 'four years of unparalleled struggling… a free and united nation… in point of freedom, no equal.' The rest of the letter (one spoke away) earns the big sentence with particulars: 'Treason walked our legislative halls,' he writes, remembering 1861's chaos, and reckons what the recovery cost. He was twenty-one when he wrote this — an age when his modern counterparts declare majors — with Antietam, Gettysburg, Lookout Mountain, Atlanta, and the March to the Sea behind him. If you keep one sentence from this archive as the answer to 'what did the soldiers think it was for,' the family's own answer is this one. And notice, one last time, WHO he's telling: Fannie. The Hubbells did their deepest thinking to each other.",
