  "location": "Near Alexandria, Virginia",
  "excerpt": "Therefore just as soon as we can make out our papers and get transportation we shall come to Ogdensburgh, and there be discharged and paid off.",
  "attribution": "Alexander, to his sister Fannie — Near Alexandria, Virginia · June 27, 1865",
  "stem": "The war is won, but Alexander is still in camp doing paperwork. What did it take to send a million-man army home?",
  "choices": [
   "Each soldier simply left when his term expired",
   "Muster-out rolls, final pay settlements, and transportation — an administrative campaign of its own",
   "A year of compulsory occupation duty for all veterans",
   "Soldiers paid their own way home and were reimbursed later"
  ],
  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. “Make out our papers” — every man's account settled: pay owed, bounty due, clothing overdrawn, then rail and steamer home to a state rendezvous for discharge. The Union demobilized some 800,000 men in about six months.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "0": "Terms had expired for thousands who still waited — no man left without his discharge in hand unless he wanted 'deserter' on the rolls forever.",
   "3": "Transportation home was the government's bill — that's the 'get transportation' he's waiting on."
  },
  "expansion": "The Union's last campaign was clerical. Every soldier's muster-out required a completed roll — five copies, error-free, reconciling three-plus years of pay, bounty installments, clothing allowances drawn against, hospital time, and detached service — before the paymaster would touch him. Regiments then traveled as units to their home-state rendezvous (Alexander's was Ogdensburgh) for final payment and discharge, so the railroads spent the summer of 1865 running the war in reverse. It worked at astonishing speed: roughly 800,000 men mustered out between May and November, the great volunteer army dissolving back into farms and shops within a year, its final ledgers accurate to the cent — arguably the young republic's most underrated administrative feat. The cultural fact rode beside the fiscal one: the United States chose, deliberately, NOT to keep a mass army in peacetime. The men wanted precisely what Alexander wants in this sentence: papers, pay, and the road home.",
  "context": "Alexander wrote this letter — his grand valediction, quoted twice in this act — from the same camps around Alexandria where the Grand Review had passed a month before, and its housekeeping sentences are as revealing as its oratory: after four years, Antietam to Atlanta to the sea, the war's actual ending is a queue for paperwork. He tells Fannie the mechanism precisely (papers → transportation → Ogdensburgh → 'discharged and paid off') because the family's planning needs the sequence — when to expect him, what money arrives with him. Ogdensburgh closes his circle exactly: it's where the 60th New York organized in 1861, when he was a seventeen-year-old assuring his mother he hadn't enlisted for novelty. The archive's last letters are full of this geometry — the war returning everyone to their starting coordinates, minus the ones it kept.",
