  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. “I will tell you all about it at the tea table” — the collection's last wartime sentence promises a story that would never need to be written down. The archive ends because the distance did.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "3": "They kept everything — that's why you're here. The family preserved and eventually transcribed the whole collection across five generations.",
   "0": "Paper was cheap again by 1865 — and he had stationery in hand (borrowed, he admits, from 'one of the boys'). What he no longer had was the need."
  },
  "expansion": "This is a lesson about sources themselves — the historian's craft of asking not just what a document says but why it exists. A letter collection is a map of separations: it begins when distance opens and dies the moment distance closes, which means the richest personal archives of the nineteenth century are byproducts of its disruptions — wars, migrations, gold rushes. It also means the record is structurally silent exactly where life is most whole: no letters describe the reunion, the tea table, the decades of ordinary proximity that followed. Historians call this a survivorship problem of a special kind — not lost evidence, but evidence never created. What the Hubbells' postwar lives left instead were the state's dry traces: pension files, census lines, cemetery stones — including the ones that let this project correct the family's own memory of a death date 160 years on. The letters end; the reckoning of them, which you are inside of right now, does not.",
  "context": "Every ending in this letter is doubled. Alexander writes from Camp Wheeler, Ogdensburgh — the ground where his regiment formed in 1861 — waiting for the last paymaster, promising the story of the 'grand reception' in person: the archive's four-year voice, signing off mid-anecdote, forever. What the tea table never got to hear: James, discharged from his own regiment, died that October on the journey home from Savannah — the war outliving its armistice by one brother. Charles carried his wounds another decade, to 1875. Frances kept every page. And so the promise in this sentence was kept in a way Alexander couldn't have meant: he DID tell it all — to us, in the 272 letters a mother saved because a seventeen-year-old once wrote 'write often, write often, often.' You've now heard the whole conversation. The finale will show you what you learned from it — and the archive's doors stay open.",
