  "answerIdx": 1,
  "whyRight": "That's the truth of it. “Waiting for the Paymaster ever since” — the war's last queue. A regiment at its home rendezvous could do nothing but drill, write letters, and watch the road for the man with the iron safe.",
  "whyWrong": {
   "0": "The muster-out rolls were done — that bureaucratic mountain was climbed at Alexandria (his June letter walks you through it). Only the money was missing.",
   "2": "Transportation had delivered them — Virginia to Ogdensburgh. The government moved the men faster than it moved their wages, to no soldier's surprise."
  },
  "expansion": "The final settlement was the one appointment no volunteer would leave without: months of arrears, the withheld bounty balance, allowances — for many men well over a hundred dollars, the largest sum they had ever held. Paymasters crisscrossed the northern states all summer with escorts and iron safes, perpetually behind schedule, while regiments idled at rendezvous camps in a purgatory every soldier's letter of July 1865 describes identically: war over, home visible, money late. (The report that some of the war's very last camps ran on borrowed stationery and patience is not apocryphal — Alexander's own letter is written on paper begged from 'one of the boys.') When the paymaster finally came, the man signed, pocketed the war's conclusion in greenbacks, and walked out a civilian. The Hubbell collection's financial thread — $8 in 1861, sutler discipline, greenbacks, arrears, bounties, a dead brother's back pay — ends here, at a pay table in Ogdensburgh, one signature from closed.",
  "context": "Twenty miles from his mother's kitchen and pinned in camp by an absent accountant — the shape of the whole war's purse, one last time: the government's promises always arrived, and always late, and the family planned around the lag to the end ('I have some doubts whether I shall be able to be home this week'). This is the same letter as the tea table — the collection's final wartime page — and it's fitting that its two surviving sentences in this game are a money complaint and a homecoming promise, because those were the twin engines of the correspondence from the first $8 forward. The teasing formality of 'the Paymaster,' capitalized like a personage of state, is Alexander's last piece of army comedy. He signed, was paid, and went home to the tea table; the ledger, and the letters, close together.",
